    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Ottine was named in 1879 by combining the names of founding settlers Adolf Otto and his wife Christine.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/ottine-tx"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Before the railroad, the settlement was called Otto's Mill and briefly Otto's Station.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/ottine-tx"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Ottine lies nine miles northwest of Gonzales in western Gonzales County.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/ottine-tx"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "By 1897 Ottine boasted two general stores, a sawmill, lumberyard, blacksmith shop, saloon, church, and a gristmill powered by the San Marcos River.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/ottine-tx"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "In 1899 local farmers in Ottine produced 7,000 bales of cotton.",
